  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 135 PM MDT Thu Sep 3 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nSouthwesterly flow will persist in the region through the weekend\nas the upper-trough continues to sit over the west coast. This \nwill allow temperatures to cool down in Central and North-Central \nMT through the weekend. This set-up will allow for afternoon \nshowers and thunderstorms, mainly in South western Montana and \nalong the Continental Divide. Additionally, there will be breezy \nsouthwest winds present, mainly impacting Southwestern Montana. \n\nAs the upper-level low moves west Sunday night into Monday, \nwidespread breezy winds will develop and continue through Tuesday.\nIn addition, the upper-level low shifting will likely bring \nwidespread showers Sunday night through Monday evening. For Sunday\nnight and Monday afternoon, there is also the chance for \nwidespread thunderstorms. As the upper-level low moves northwest \nand out of the region, the showers will taper off over Tuesday \nnight. -EH\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nWinds on Thursday and Friday:\n\nBreezy southwesterly winds will be possible across Southwest \nMontana this afternoon with afternoon relative humidity in the 20s\nexpected. This will keep fire weather concerns elevated for \nportions of Southwest Montana where a Red Flag Warning remains in \neffect through the evening. While stronger winds are possible \nalong north-south oriented valleys including Ennis, there is low \nconfidence in reaching high wind criteria with gusts expected to \nmax out at around 45 mph. Across the rest of the plains and \nCentral Montana, gusts between 25 and 35 mph are likely but much \nstronger gusts are not expected. A similar setup is expected\ntomorrow with scattered thunderstorms possible which may bring \nlocally stronger wind gusts.\n\n\nRain Sunday through Tuesday:\n\nBased on the latest model guidance, precipitation starts to fill \nin across the region Sunday morning into the afternoon with the \nheaviest moisture expected on Monday as the shortwave passes \nthrough. Lingering showers will continue through Tuesday morning \nand taper off from south to north through the afternoon. There is \nstill uncertainty regarding precipitation amounts but right now \nthe Hi- Line is the most favored for higher amounts while \nSouthwest Montana is more favored for the low-end amounts.\n\nOver the 48 hour period covering Sunday morning through Tuesday \nmorning...\n\n - For amounts greater than 0.1\" there is a 80% probability\n   of occurrence across the region.\n\n - For amounts greater than 0.5\" there is a 75% probability of \n   occurrence north of a line between Helena and White Sulphur \n   Springs and a 50% chance south of that line.\n\n - For amounts greater than 1.0\" there is a 50% probability\n   of occurrence north of a line between Helena and White Sulphur\n   Springs and a 25% chance south of that line.\n\n - For amounts greater than 1.5\" there is a 40% probability of occurrence\n   north of a line between Great Falls and Lewistown and a less \n   than 15% chance south of that line.\n\nExact amounts are likely to change but there is increasing \nconfidence that this shortwave will at least bring minor relief to\nsome of the recent fire weather concerns. -thor",
